 ## 6. Statistical methods / specifications
-The preferred decomposition uses a 30-year monthly moving average lagged by one year:
 
+**Main estimating equation (Eq. 13)**:
+
 $$
-\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}} = \frac{1}{30} \sum_{j=y-30}^{y-1} \bar{x}_{imj},
+\text{Ozone}_{it} = \beta_W \text{Temp}_{it}^W + \beta_C \bar{\text{Temp}}_{i\bar{p}}^C + X_{it}'\delta + \phi_{is} + \varepsilon_{it}
 $$
 
-with $\bar{x}_{imj}$ the monthly mean for month $m$ in year $j$ at monitor $i$. Implicitly the weights $\omega_j = 1$ for $j \in \{y-30, \ldots, y-1\}$ and zero otherwise. Monthly granularity is chosen because agents are assumed to recall climate by month rather than by day-of-year (this is partly defended on observation: weather media commonly report monthly highs and lows over the past 10/20/30 years).
+where $\phi_{is}$ = monitor-by-season-by-year FE; $X_{it}$ includes decomposed precipitation controls (norm + shock); standard errors clustered at county level.
 
-The general weighting form is:
+**FWL equivalences (Eqs. 5–8)**:
+- Eq. (5): Ideal unifying equation — $y_{it} = \alpha + \beta_W(x_{it} - \bar{x}_i) + \beta_C \bar{x}_i + \mu_i + \lambda_t + \nu_{it}$
+- Eq. (6): Feasible unifying equation — $y_{it} = \alpha + \beta_W(x_{it} - \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}) + \beta_C \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}} + \mu_i + \lambda_s + \nu_{it}$
+- Eq. (7): Time-averaged → recovers $\beta_C$
+- Eq. (8): Within-transformation → recovers $\beta_W$
 
+**CS and FE as limiting cases**:
+- Eq. (1): $y_i = \alpha + \beta_{CS} x_i + e_i$ (cross-section)
+- Eq. (2): $y_{it} = \alpha + \beta_{FE} x_{it} + \mu_i + \lambda_t + \nu_{it}$ (panel FE)
+
+**Climate norm construction (Eq. 12)**:
 $$
-\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}} \equiv \frac{1}{J} \sum_{j=1}^{J<y} \omega_j \bar{x}_{imj} \approx \bar{x}_{imy},
+\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}} = \frac{1}{30}\sum_{j=y-30}^{y-1} \bar{x}_{imj}
 $$
+(30-year monthly MA, lagged 1 year)
 
-which the authors note can be specialized to other filtering schemes (Baxter and King 1999; Christiano and Fitzgerald 2003).
+**Nonlinear extensions**:
+- Quadratic (Eq. 14): adds $\beta_{W2}(x_{it} - \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}})^2 + \beta_{C2}(\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}})^2$
+- Marginal adaptation under quadratic (Eq. 15): $(\beta_W - \beta_C) + 2[\beta_{W2}(x_{it} - \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}) - \beta_{C2}\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}]$
+- Marginal adaptation at zero shock (Eq. 16): $\beta_W - \beta_C - 2\beta_{C2}\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}$
+- Cubic: adds $\beta_{W3}(x_{it} - \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}})^3 + \beta_{C3}(\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}})^3$
+- Binned: 5 °C bins; lowest $< 20$ °C, highest $> 35$ °C, middle bin 25–30 °C (median 27.8 °C, mean 27.1 °C)
 
-The main estimating equation is:
+**OAD short-run robustness**: adds $\text{Shock} \times \mathbf{1}[\text{Action Day}_{it}]$ interaction to Eq. (13).
 
-$$
-Ozone_{it} = \beta_W \, Temp_{it}^W + \beta_C \, Temp_{i\bar{p}}^C + X_{it}'\delta + \phi_{is} + \epsilon_{it},
-$$
+## 7. Findings
 
-where $Temp_{it}^W = x_{it} - \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}$ is the temperature shock, $Temp_{i\bar{p}}^C = \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}$ is the climate norm, $X_{it}$ contains a similar decomposition of precipitation, $\phi_{is}$ is monitor-by-season-by-year fixed effects, and $s$ indexes season-of-the-sample (Spring or Summer in each year).
+**Primary results (Table 1, col 1)**:
+- Temperature shock $\hat{\beta}_W = 1.678$ ppb/°C (SE 0.063; $p < 0.01$)
+- Climate norm $\hat{\beta}_C = 1.164$ ppb/°C (SE 0.051; $p < 0.01$)
+- Implied adaptation $= 0.514$ ppb/°C (SE 0.041; $p < 0.01$)
 
-**Fixed-effects structure.** Monitor-by-season-by-year fixed effects $\phi_{is}$ replace the separate monitor $\mu_i$ and time $\lambda_s$ fixed effects of the general theoretical equation. The authors note results are quantitatively similar with separated $\mu_i$ and $\lambda_s$; they combine them to control more flexibly for local factors changing across seasons and years. Analogous to Isen et al. (2017), this is equivalent to estimating the main specification monitor-by-monitor in each season-year and taking the weighted average of all coefficients. The Appendix C derivation shows that two sources of monitor-level variation in $\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}$ — within-season (across months) and across-year (year-to-year MA updates) — survive within this fixed-effects structure.
+**Benchmarks**:
+- Panel FE (col 2): $\hat{\beta}_{FE} = 1.659$ ppb (SE 0.063) — statistically indistinguishable from $\hat{\beta}_W$ ✓
+- Cross-section (col 3): $\hat{\beta}_{CS} = 1.166$ ppb (SE 0.106) — statistically indistinguishable from $\hat{\beta}_C$ ✓ (consistent with FWL)
 
-**Why FWL.** The reason a separate first-stage de-seasonalization is unnecessary on the outcome variable is Lovell's Theorem 4.1, point 4: it is immaterial whether the dependent variable is adjusted, provided the explanatory variables have been seasonally corrected. This is what permits estimating both effects in the same equation.
+**Climate penalty projections (RCP 8.5)**:
+- Correct (norm-based): +1.9 ppb by 2050, +5.6 ppb by 2100
+- Incorrect (shock-based): +2.7 ppb by 2050, +8.0 ppb by 2100
+- Overestimation: +44% in relative terms; +0.82 ppb by 2050, +2.47 ppb by 2100
 
-**Standard errors.** Clustered at the county level. Bootstrap and state-level clustering are explored in Appendix B Table B5 with similar inference.
+**Robustness (Table 2)**:
+- Adaptation stable across MA lengths: 0.495–0.542 ppb over 3–30 year windows
+- Longer lag structures (10- and 20-year lags): adaptation 0.527–0.542 ppb (slightly larger)
+- OAD interaction: Shock × Action Day = 0.068 (SE 0.188; insignificant) → no short-run adaptive response
 
-**Comparison equations.** The cross-sectional benchmark is $y_i = \alpha + \beta_{CS} x_i + e_i$ (Eq. 1). The standard panel benchmark is $y_{it} = \alpha + \beta_{FE} x_{it} + \mu_i + \lambda_t + \nu_{it}$ (Eq. 2). The ideal-but-infeasible equation embedding both is $y_{it} = \alpha + \beta_W(x_{it} - \bar{x}_i) + \beta_C \bar{x}_i + \mu_i + \lambda_t + \nu_{it}$ (Eq. 5), which fails because $\bar{x}_i$ is collinear with $\mu_i$. The feasible approximation (Eq. 6) breaks this collinearity by letting the norm vary across time via the lagged historical MA.
+**Nonlinear results (Fig. 4)**:
+- Ozone-temperature response: increasing at increasing rate at low temperatures, increasing at decreasing rate at high temperatures
+- Marginal adaptation (binned/cubic): "normal u" shape — greatest adaptive effort at highest temperatures
+- Linear spec is a good first-order approximation; quadratic misspecifies; cubic and binned preferred for nonlinear analysis
 
-**Nonlinear extension.** A quadratic version adds $(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}})^2$ and $\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}^2$ terms; cubic adds the cube. A binned linear version interacts the shock, norm, and controls with 5-degree-Celsius temperature-bin indicators. Marginal adaptation in the quadratic case is $(\beta_W - \beta_C) + 2(\beta_{W2}(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}) - \beta_{C2}\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}})$.
+**Heterogeneity (Appendix B.2)**:
+- Adaptation measure relatively stable over time (Fig. B1), though the level effect of climate norm on ozone has decreased over time
+- Additional heterogeneity by belief in climate change (Tables B9–B11) and NOx/VOC precursor limitation (Table B12)
 
