+## 8. Contributions
 
-- Temperature shock: $\hat{\beta}_W = 1.678$ ppb per degree Celsius (SE 0.063), highly significant.
-- Climate norm: $\hat{\beta}_C = 1.164$ ppb per degree Celsius (SE 0.051), highly significant.
-- Implied adaptation: $\hat{\beta}_W - \hat{\beta}_C = 0.514$ ppb per degree Celsius (SE 0.041), highly significant.
-- Benchmark fixed-effects on raw temperature (column 2): 1.659 (SE 0.063) — statistically equivalent to $\hat{\beta}_W$.
-- Benchmark cross-section (column 3): 1.166 (SE 0.106) — statistically equivalent to $\hat{\beta}_C$.
+1. **Unifying framework**: Nests the cross-sectional (Mendelsohn et al. 1994; Schlenker et al. 2005) and panel FE (Deschenes-Greenstone 2007; Schlenker-Roberts 2009) approaches in a single estimating equation.
+2. **Direct, testable adaptation measure**: $\hat{\beta}_W - \hat{\beta}_C$ with standard errors from the same regression — no SUR, no bootstrap, no resampling required.
+3. **Overcomes OVB of the cross-section**: The FWL decomposition eliminates the confounders that plague a pure cross-sectional approach by including monitor FE.
+4. **Overcomes the Lucas Critique**: Comparing weather responses across time or space implicitly assumes structural stability; the norm/shock decomposition compares the same agents' long- vs. short-run responses.
+5. **Applied insight for ozone**: Shows that omitting adaptation leads to ~44% overestimation of the climate penalty, with implications for EPA ozone standard-setting and air quality projections.
 
-**Implied climate penalty.** Using Vose et al. (2017) RCP 8.5 projections (1.6 C by 2050, 4.8 C by 2100), the unifying approach predicts ozone increases of 1.9 ppb (2050) and 5.6 ppb (2100). Using $\hat{\beta}_W$ instead (the no-adaptation counterfactual) would over-predict the penalty as 2.7 and 8 ppb. Disregarding adaptation overstates the penalty by roughly 44%.
+## 9. Replication feasibility
 
-**Robustness (Table 2):**
-- 3/5/10/20/30-year MAs yield $\hat{\beta}_W$ from 1.669–1.678 and $\hat{\beta}_C$ from 1.158–1.176, with adaptation 0.495–0.514. Stability of the norm coefficient across MA windows suggests 20–30-year windows are near-optimal for the bias trade-off (Solon 1992 — measurement error vs. fixed-effects-induced attenuation).
-- 10-year and 20-year lags between the MA and contemporaneous temperature yield adaptation 0.527 and 0.542, slightly larger as expected when agents have more time to adjust.
-- 2004–2013 subsample with OAD interactions: shock × Ozone Action Day = 0.068 (SE 0.188), statistically zero. The authors interpret this as evidence that short-run adaptation is small relative to long-run adaptation. (For the JMP, this is the "Bento test" — alert effects do not show up at this level of aggregation in this paper's design, but the JMP refines the design to look for them.)
+- Data: Publicly available (EPA AQS ozone monitors; NOAA GHCN temperature; EPA OAD alerts from 2004)
+- Replication archive: https://doi.org/10.3886/E192708V1 (openICPSR)
+- Online appendix (including appendix tables and figures): https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeem.2023.102843
+- Sample period: 1980–2013; N = 5,139,523 monitor-day observations
+- Standard panel FE software (Stata/R) is sufficient for main specification
 
-**Nonlinear specifications (Figure 4 and Appendix Table B6).** The linear specification captures first-order nonlinearity well. The cubic and binned specifications both show "inverted-U" marginal effects of temperature on ozone — ozone increases at a decreasing rate at higher temperatures — and "normal-U" marginal adaptation — adaptation is larger when temperature is hotter. The authors interpret this as agents focusing adaptive effort on the days that would otherwise produce the most ozone.
