-## 8. Contributions
-1. **Joint identification in one equation.** The method recovers short- and long-run climate impacts in the same panel fixed-effects regression, sidestepping the SUR/bootstrap acrobatics needed when the cross-sectional and panel coefficients come from separate equations (Dell, Jones, Olken 2012, 2014).
-2. **Direct measure of adaptation with a standard hypothesis test.** Because both coefficients are estimated in one equation, testing $H_0: \beta_W = \beta_C$ is trivial — a single linear restriction. SUR or block-bootstrap is not needed.
-3. **Reduced OVB exposure for the long-run estimate.** Unlike pure cross-sectional climate regressions, the long-run norm coefficient here is identified within unit fixed effects, exploiting (i) the year-to-year update of the MA and (ii) within-season-year monthly variation in the norm. This eliminates between-monitor OVB sources.
-4. **Application to the "climate penalty" on ozone.** First quasi-experimental estimate of climate-induced adaptation in ozone formation, suggesting modeled climate-penalty projections (Jacob and Winner 2009; USEPA 2009) may overshoot by ignoring behavioral response.
-5. **Generalizable framework.** Any climate-related outcome (agriculture, mortality, electricity demand, labor productivity) admits the same decomposition.
