-- One-liner: Stylized envelope diagram of cost-minimizing production schedules as temperature rises, illustrating the difference between a transitory shock (point A to A', no adaptation) and a permanent norm change (point A to C, adaptation through switching to schedule 2). The schematic motivates why $\beta_W$ identifies the no-adaptation response and $\beta_C$ identifies the adaptation-inclusive response.
-- Tier B (schematic — theoretical model diagram, not a data figure).
-- **Figure notes:** Cost of production on the left y-axis, associated ozone concentration on the right y-axis, temperature on the x-axis. Production schedule 1 dominates between $T_1$ and $T_2$, schedule 2 between $T_2$ and $T_3$, with indifference at $T_2$ (point B). A firm at climate-norm temperature $T_1$ produces at A; under a transitory shock $T_3$ it produces at A' on schedule 1 (no adaptation), but under permanent norm change to $T_3$ it switches to schedule 2 and produces at C. The gray dashed line is the long-run equilibrium envelope (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
