-- Type: line plot, two-panel comparison.
-- X-axis: day of year, 2013 ozone season (Los Angeles).
-- Y-axis: temperature (degrees Celsius).
-- Series / panels: Panel A — daily max temperature (dashed top line), 30-year monthly MA climate norm (solid black line), and temperature shock (solid gray line at bottom). Panel B — same dashed top line, but with a fixed-effects-decomposition mean (solid black, constant within month) and deviation (solid gray) under a standard monitor-by-month decomposition.
-- Key visual finding: The temperature shocks in Panel A (paper's preferred decomposition) are visually nearly identical to the deviations in Panel B (fixed-effects decomposition), confirming the Frisch–Waugh–Lovell equivalence. The crucial difference is that Panel A's climate norm varies smoothly across days within the season (and across years), while Panel B's fixed-effects mean is a within-month constant collinear with the LA fixed effect, providing no variation for identifying a long-run effect.
-- Annotations: dashed line at the top of each panel for observed daily max temperature, solid black line for long-run norms, solid gray line at the bottom for shocks.
-- **Figure notes:** Panel A is the paper's Eq. (6); Panel B is the standard fixed-effects decomposition of Eq. (2). The figure highlights why the unifying approach can identify both $\beta_W$ and $\beta_C$, while the fixed-effects approach can only identify $\beta_W$ (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
